1
00:00:00,151 --> 00:00:02,717
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:02,926 --> 00:00:06,571
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:06,653 --> 00:00:10,681
A twelve point one, Factors Affecting Rates of Chemical

4
00:00:10,762 --> 00:00:11,587
Reactions.

5
00:00:11,831 --> 00:00:14,965
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain

6
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:18,808
how surface area, concentration and pressure change

7
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:23,058
rate, using collision theory, and identify control variables

8
00:00:23,139 --> 00:00:24,730
in a rate investigation.

9
00:00:25,252 --> 00:00:26,901
Retrieve collision theory.

10
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:30,743
A successful collision needs sufficient energy and the correct

11
00:00:30,836 --> 00:00:32,392
spatial orientation.

12
00:00:32,844 --> 00:00:36,711
Reaction rate depends on how frequently those successful collisions

13
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:38,359
occur per unit time.

14
00:00:38,882 --> 00:00:42,817
The common idea across A twelve point one is

15
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:44,176
collision frequency.

16
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:48,239
If reacting particles collide more often, there are more

17
00:00:48,285 --> 00:00:52,349
opportunities for successful collisions, so rate increases.

18
00:00:52,628 --> 00:00:54,915
Energy and orientation still matter.

19
00:00:55,251 --> 00:00:58,920
More collisions does not mean every collision is successful.

20
00:00:59,094 --> 00:01:03,227
Specification point A twelve point one point one: surface

21
00:01:03,286 --> 00:01:03,854
area.

22
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:08,057
For the same mass of solid, smaller pieces have a greater total

23
00:01:08,126 --> 00:01:09,578
exposed surface area.

24
00:01:09,694 --> 00:01:13,432
Only surface particles are directly available for collisions

25
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:14,837
with the other reactant.

26
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:16,579
Watch the particle models.

27
00:01:16,822 --> 00:01:19,388
Large chips expose less surface.

28
00:01:19,481 --> 00:01:21,362
Small chips expose more.

29
00:01:21,698 --> 00:01:24,891
Powder exposes the most surface for the same mass.

30
00:01:25,042 --> 00:01:28,850
The teacher PowerPoint uses two experiments, both with two

31
00:01:28,931 --> 00:01:30,766
point zero grams of solid.

32
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:34,748
Experiment A, large chips, reaches the endpoint in

33
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:36,176
ninety five seconds.

34
00:01:36,419 --> 00:01:40,332
Experiment B, small chips, reaches the same endpoint

35
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:41,748
in forty eight seconds.

36
00:01:42,085 --> 00:01:43,374
B is faster.

37
00:01:43,618 --> 00:01:47,379
The same mass of small chips has a greater total surface area,

38
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:50,874
so collision frequency at the solid surface is higher.

39
00:01:51,268 --> 00:01:54,276
The student workbook uses a third trial as well.

40
00:01:54,671 --> 00:01:56,900
Large chips, ninety six seconds.

41
00:01:57,144 --> 00:01:59,547
Small chips, fifty one seconds.

42
00:01:59,884 --> 00:02:01,834
Powder, twenty nine seconds.

43
00:02:02,229 --> 00:02:05,851
Those times are not the same as the PowerPoint pair, so keep

44
00:02:05,909 --> 00:02:07,511
the two datasets separate.

45
00:02:07,848 --> 00:02:11,958
The scientific pattern is the same: as particle size decreases,

46
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:14,407
time to the same endpoint decreases.

47
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,413
A common error is to say that smaller particles contain

48
00:02:18,506 --> 00:02:19,516
more substance.

49
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:23,103
For the same mass, the amount of substance is the same.

50
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:26,331
What changes is the exposed surface area.

51
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:31,091
Now specification point A twelve point one point two: concentration.

52
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:35,120
Concentration is how much dissolved reactant is present in

53
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:36,002
a given volume.

54
00:02:36,397 --> 00:02:40,019
At higher concentration there are more reactant particles

55
00:02:40,135 --> 00:02:41,307
per unit volume.

56
00:02:41,644 --> 00:02:45,545
Temperature is unchanged, so the particles do not need to move faster.

57
00:02:45,824 --> 00:02:49,922
They simply meet more often because they are packed more closely.

58
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:54,241
The shared concentration dataset is: zero point five

59
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:58,107
zero moles per cubic decimetre takes one hundred and twenty

60
00:02:58,165 --> 00:03:02,008
seconds; one point zero zero takes sixty four seconds;

61
00:03:02,182 --> 00:03:05,793
one point five zero takes forty three seconds; two point

62
00:03:05,839 --> 00:03:08,289
zero zero takes thirty one seconds.

63
00:03:08,684 --> 00:03:12,875
Higher concentration means fewer seconds to the same endpoint,

64
00:03:13,049 --> 00:03:14,372
so rate increases.

65
00:03:14,547 --> 00:03:18,239
Do not assume that doubling concentration exactly doubles rate

66
00:03:18,448 --> 00:03:20,677
unless the evidence says so.

67
00:03:21,071 --> 00:03:25,472
Specification point A twelve point one point three: pressure,

68
00:03:25,553 --> 00:03:26,458
for gases.

69
00:03:26,853 --> 00:03:30,789
At constant temperature, increasing pressure can put the same

70
00:03:30,835 --> 00:03:33,680
number of gas particles in a smaller volume.

71
00:03:34,074 --> 00:03:38,126
The particles are closer together, so there are more particles

72
00:03:38,196 --> 00:03:41,644
per unit volume and collisions become more frequent.

73
00:03:42,259 --> 00:03:45,905
Do not claim that average kinetic energy has increased

74
00:03:45,975 --> 00:03:47,589
if temperature is unchanged.

75
00:03:47,983 --> 00:03:51,873
Pressure can rise by reducing volume at constant temperature.

76
00:03:52,326 --> 00:03:54,798
That is not the same as heating the mixture.

77
00:03:55,135 --> 00:03:59,013
On a gas-volume versus time graph, a faster reaction

78
00:03:59,082 --> 00:04:00,917
has a steeper initial curve.

79
00:04:01,253 --> 00:04:04,934
If the same limiting amount reacts, the final product amount

80
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,896
can be the same, reached sooner.

81
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:10,825
When you plan an investigation, change only the intended factor.

82
00:04:11,069 --> 00:04:14,888
For surface area, control mass of solid, concentration

83
00:04:14,958 --> 00:04:18,522
and volume of the other reactant, temperature and apparatus.

84
00:04:18,696 --> 00:04:22,632
For concentration, also control the surface area or mass

85
00:04:22,748 --> 00:04:23,816
of any solid.

86
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:25,500
Correct the misconceptions.

87
00:04:25,778 --> 00:04:28,460
Smaller pieces do not contain more substance.

88
00:04:28,704 --> 00:04:32,477
Higher concentration does not make particles faster if temperature

89
00:04:32,524 --> 00:04:33,394
is unchanged.

90
00:04:33,638 --> 00:04:37,121
Higher gas pressure does not always mean higher temperature.

91
00:04:37,295 --> 00:04:40,662
And more collisions does not make every collision successful.

92
00:04:40,999 --> 00:04:42,926
Examination-style question.

93
00:04:43,135 --> 00:04:44,110
Part a.

94
00:04:44,284 --> 00:04:48,023
Explain why a powdered solid reacts faster than the same

95
00:04:48,069 --> 00:04:49,509
mass of large pieces.

96
00:04:49,752 --> 00:04:50,728
Three marks.

97
00:04:50,902 --> 00:04:53,758
Powder has a greater total surface area.

98
00:04:54,036 --> 00:04:56,753
Collision frequency at the surface increases.

99
00:04:57,032 --> 00:05:00,770
Successful collisions occur more frequently, so rate

100
00:05:00,828 --> 00:05:01,664
increases.

101
00:05:01,838 --> 00:05:02,581
Part b.

102
00:05:02,825 --> 00:05:06,470
Explain why increasing reactant concentration increases

103
00:05:06,563 --> 00:05:07,028
rate.

104
00:05:07,237 --> 00:05:08,084
Three marks.

105
00:05:08,235 --> 00:05:10,778
There are more particles per unit volume.

106
00:05:10,987 --> 00:05:12,856
Collision frequency increases.

107
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:16,269
Successful collisions per unit time increase.

108
00:05:16,478 --> 00:05:17,268
Part c.

109
00:05:17,477 --> 00:05:21,412
Explain why increasing the pressure of gaseous reactants increases

110
00:05:21,529 --> 00:05:21,947
rate.

111
00:05:22,121 --> 00:05:22,991
Three marks.

112
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:26,916
At constant temperature the same particles occupy a smaller

113
00:05:26,974 --> 00:05:29,087
volume, so they are closer together.

114
00:05:29,296 --> 00:05:33,150
Collision frequency increases, and successful collisions occur

115
00:05:33,266 --> 00:05:34,230
more frequently.

116
00:05:34,474 --> 00:05:35,112
Part d.

117
00:05:35,391 --> 00:05:39,210
State one control variable for a surface-area investigation

118
00:05:39,419 --> 00:05:41,463
and explain why you control it.

119
00:05:41,741 --> 00:05:42,531
Two marks.

120
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:46,524
Control the mass of solid, or the concentration of the other

121
00:05:46,583 --> 00:05:48,161
reactant, or temperature.

122
00:05:48,370 --> 00:05:52,365
That isolates surface area so the comparison is valid.

123
00:05:52,701 --> 00:05:55,151
Before we finish, complete the model.

124
00:05:55,488 --> 00:05:59,296
Surface area, concentration and pressure all change how

125
00:05:59,366 --> 00:06:00,875
often particles collide.

126
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:05,101
More frequent collisions give more successful collisions per unit

127
00:06:05,147 --> 00:06:07,121
time, so rate increases.

128
00:06:07,365 --> 00:06:11,730
Energy and orientation still decide which collisions succeed.

